> +static void ctlv_message_output_frag_mark(struct ctlv_op_ctx *ctx);

Forward declaration of a static function that is defined at the very end of the file. Move the definition above `ctlv_op_query_more()` and drop the prototype.


This is the serious design problem of the patch. The loop dereferences every cursor from `ctx->deepest` up through ->up. Those are `struct ctlv_cursor` objects on the stack frames of the family's (helper) functions. The header says "`ctlv_op_query_more()` marks still-open nests, whose cursors must remain live", but the normal way to reach this path is exactly the one where they are not: a put or `nest_start` fails because the reply buffer is full (not poisoned for queries, see `ctlv_message_output_full()`), the helper returns false, its frame is popped (taking its nest cursor with it), and the top-level query callback then calls `ctlv_op_query_more()`. `ctx->deepest` still points into the dead frame. `ctlv_op_query_done()` carefully avoids following such a pointer ("A cursor of a frame that has returned is not followed"), but this function follows it unconditionally, reading ->start and ->up from reused stack and OR-ing `FRAG_MORE` into root + a garbage offset: a kernel memory write at a stack-derived offset.

Please make this robust by construction: e.g. keep the open-nest offsets in the ctx itself (a depth-indexed array of `u32`, depth <= `CTLV_MAX_NEST_DEPTH`) so `frag_mark` needs no cursor pointers, and ->deepest becomes a depth. That also removes the "deepest != nest" WARNs and the pointer-identity tricks in `ctlv_op_query_done()` and `core.c`'s `ctlv_reply_finish()`.

Also confirm the validator and the ABI doc agree that `FRAG_MORE` is set on the root attr as well.

